  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The right approach is <b>Start by monitoring, then deploy the function, validate, and shift traffic</b>. AWS CodeDeploy for Lambda uses a sequence designed for safe and controlled deployments: monitoring health before and after changes, deploying the new version, validating the function (using test traffic or hooks), then gradually shifting live traffic to the new version. This minimizes risk and allows issues to be caught early.</p><ul><li><i>Deploy the Lambda function, then validate, shift traffic, and monitor</i> puts deployment before monitoring and validation, which can cause missed issues or failed rollbacks.</li><li><i>Begin by monitoring, then shift traffic, deploy the function, and validate</i> incorrectly shifts traffic before updating the function, which is not possible.</li><li><i>Initiate traffic shifting, validate the function, monitor, and finally deploy</i> is out of order—traffic shifting must come after a successful deployment and validation.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> Always monitor first, then deploy, validate with pre-traffic/post-traffic hooks, and shift traffic last to reduce user impact.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are the DevOps engineer at an AI-driven enterprise, where you oversee Lambda function deployments using AWS CodeDeploy. Each week, a fresh build is ready for deployment and you need to manage the sequence of CodeDeploy steps. Which sequence should you follow?</p>",
